00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:09.440
the supplies he was also the one who knew how to&nbsp;
fix the sledges and he was the only one who knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:15.440
how to speak with he knew it if anything henson&nbsp;
was the one who didn't need peri on april 6 1909&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:19.440
the team made camp for the last time on their way&nbsp;
to the pole up until this point their camps were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.240
simply named camp number one camp number two&nbsp;
etc but they felt that something special was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.120
needed to mark this momentous occasion therefore&nbsp;
the commander took an american silk flag and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:34.320
carefully planted it on top of their igloo&nbsp;
christening the location camp morris k jessup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:39.360
after one of their financiers the six man went to&nbsp;
sleep and woke up the next morning ready to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:44.400
final march to the north pole ostensibly the plan&nbsp;
was for the faster henson to scout ahead and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:48.480
allow the navy officer to overtake at the&nbsp;
last minute and claim the glory of being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:53.520
first to the north pole however henson's team&nbsp;
overshot their target let's say by mistake but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:58.000
who knows so when perry finally caught up&nbsp;
to them matthienson greeted him by saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:02.720
i think i'm the first man to sit on top of the&nbsp;
world according to henson's journal the mood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:08.240
became decidedly frosty from that point on even by&nbsp;
arctic standards henson wrote from the time of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:13.840
arrival at the roosevelt for nearly three weeks my&nbsp;
days were spent in complete idleness i would catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:19.680
a fleeting glimpse of commander peri but not once&nbsp;
in all that time did he speak a word to me then he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:24.640
spoke to me in the most ordinary matter-of-fact&nbsp;
way and ordered me to get to work not a word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:28.960
about the north pole or anything connected with&nbsp;
it even though neither one said anything about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:33.600
it they both knew that the partnership they&nbsp;
had had for over 20 years had disintegrated

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:43.120
and piri immediately went to work to ensure that&nbsp;
he was the one who enjoyed all the fame and glory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:47.600
and not matthew henson this did not prove&nbsp;
difficult despite his decades of experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:52.720
and success racial prejudices against henson&nbsp;
were still pervasive and the world at large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:57.360
was more than happy to dismiss him as simply a&nbsp;
mere sidekick to the commander even newspapers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:01.520
of the day decried the fact that perry gave the&nbsp;
honor of reaching the north pole to a black man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:05.200
instead of one of his white companions&nbsp;
such as the ship's captain robert bartlett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:09.360
only other explorers who knew matthienson&nbsp;
personally understood how indispensable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:14.080
he'd truly been to the success of the mission&nbsp;
and the peri didn't take him by choice but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:18.240
because he could not have done it without&nbsp;
him even after he published his own memoir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:25.200
in 1912 titled a negro explorer at the north pole&nbsp;
henson still went mostly ignored however karma is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:30.160
you know what and peary encountered a completely&nbsp;
different and unexpected opposition to his claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:34.400
frederick cook a man who accompanied them&nbsp;
on henson's first trip to the arctic as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:39.200
team's surgeon he also claimed to have reached the&nbsp;
north pole and he said that he did it first this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:43.760
triggered an intense debate over who was telling&nbsp;
the truth if indeed any of them were whether by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:48.480
choice or by force matthew henson mostly stayed&nbsp;
out of the controversy although he disbelieved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:53.440
cook's story feeling that he was too soft to&nbsp;
succeed in such a grueling mission as far as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:57.920
henson was concerned he lived a quiet unassuming&nbsp;
life after his arctic days were over he never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:02.720
tried to go on another mission both because he did&nbsp;
not want to do it without his long-term companion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.880
and because he felt that he'd already accomplished&nbsp;
what he set out to do in the arctic henson found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:10.960
a job with the u.s customs house in new york&nbsp;
city on recommendation from president teddy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:15.520
roosevelt himself and he spent almost 30 years&nbsp;
working there he had married again to a woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:22.160
named lucy ross in 1907 and had an inuit concubine&nbsp;
named akantinwa while in greenland who gave him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:29.040
his only biological child a son named anaok this&nbsp;
fact however was not publicized until the 1950s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:33.520
after henson's death unfortunately in the decades&nbsp;
following his historic trip to the north pole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:38.080
matthiensen saw his accomplishments swept under&nbsp;
the rug and forgotten while his former partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:43.280
was made an admiral and received multiple medals&nbsp;
and honors it wasn't until 1937 that henson was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:47.680
made an honorary member of the explorers club of&nbsp;
new york and a decade later he received a medal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:52.400
by the u.s navy for his efforts mathienson's&nbsp;
final tribute in his lifetime came in 1954 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:56.400
president dwight eisenhower invited him to the&nbsp;
white house to receive a special commendation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:00.960
for his work as an explorer matthienson&nbsp;
died a year later on march 9 1955 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:06.720
the bronx age 88 in 1988 following a special&nbsp;
exception granted by president ronald reagan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:11.440
matthew henson and his wife lucy were re-buried&nbsp;
at the arlington national cemetery with full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:15.680
military honours the image showed henson and the&nbsp;
four men who accompanied him to the frozen edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:21.040
of the earth thus some long deserved recognition&nbsp;
was finally granted to five intrepid explorers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:27.840
persevered and triumphed in the face of adversity&nbsp;
both from mother nature and their fellow men

